David Justiss <dajustis@hotmail.com> deserves a cookie for saying:
> I'm going to list some monsters from nethack that I don't know what
> stories or legends they are from, anybody with more information about
> these monsters, please tell me.  I'm interested in stuff like mythology,
> fairy tales and fantasy stories and want to find out where these
> monsters came from:

> leocrotta

Usually known as a "Leucrotta."  It's a mythical beast of European
origin with the head of a badger (or lion) and body of a stag.  

> quasit and homunculus

A quasit is a demonic/devilish creature, similar to, but nastier than an
imp, often used as a familiar.  Like imps, they can also shapechange,
but unlike imps, quasits are highly poisonous.  Dunno of the origin.
Perhaps Gygaxian.  

Homunculi are like tiny golems, appearing human apart from their small
size.  They are artificial, created by a wizard or similar person to do
tasks or protect.  The concept of the golem is ages old, of course, but
the homunculus in particular may have been devised by Paracelsus.
Regardless, it's mythically European in origin.  Many variations exist,
including homunculi that appear impish or are of massive size.  

> vrock

A weak demon resembling a humanoid vulture.  Think of the skekses from
Dark Crystal.  Likely Gygaxian/D&D in origin.

> lich

Extremely powerful undead.  Resembles a mummy or zombie in that it's
extremely dessicated and rotten, but moves more gracefully and normally.
Liches are wizards that have purposefully turned themselves undead to
attain power and immortality.  The concept is likely European in origin,
if not in name.  The traditional Lich everyone is familiar with is
Gygaxian.  

> zruty

Slovakian Yeti/Bigfoot, I believe.  

> xorn

Another one you can thank Gygax for.  A tri-armed, tri-legged creature
with a mouth on the top of its head.  It burrows head-first through
stone.  

> tengu

Japanese goblins.  Half-bird, half-human, able to change shape.  

> aleax

Either a god's vengeance given form or a creature employed/summoned by a
god to exact vengeance.  It appears essentially identical to the target.
Not sure of the origin.

> hezrou

Yet another D&D demon.  This one is frog-like.

> lemure

Heh.  Depends.  There is a D&D lemure.  It's a very weak devil, a
vaguely humanoid blob of molten flesh.  

However, a lemure is also a real animal.  A fuzzy, little, tree-hanging
prosimian with big eyes and a long fluffy tail.  

> rothe (is it a real animal?)

Mythical oxen.  Fairly big with large, curved horns and shaggy hair.  
Dunno the origin.

> shrieker (is the quote the game gives from the origonal source?)

A big purple mushroom that screams.  That's all.  Gygaxian, I'm fairly
sure.  

> umber hulk (or did the maker of hack just need something for capital U?)

Again Gygaxian, it's a large beetle-primate.  Body of a large ape, head
of a beetle, complete with mandibles.  

> purple worm

Just a really big, really purple worm that eats things.  Probably
Gygaxian.

